也许是因为他躲在角落里太远了。

603
00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:28.740
Yeah.
是啊。

604
00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.580
It just traded between me and Oscar.
这事儿就在我和奥斯卡之间传开了。

605
00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:37.680
Well, did you notice in this scene in Stanford when Jim wheels his chair over to the copier?
对了，你们注意到斯坦福分部那场戏里，吉姆把椅子滑到复印机旁边的镜头了吗？

606
00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:39.040
Did you look in the background?
你们看背景了吗？

607
00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.660
I noticed a little something back there.
我在那儿发现了一点小细节。

608
00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.880
I saw a few people write in.
我看到有几个人写信问过。

609
00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:46.840
You have to tell them because I think this is so beautiful.
你一定要告诉他们，因为我觉得这太美好了。

610
00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:48.420
All right.
好吧。

611
00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:52.160
I also talked to Randall about this, and he said this was totally on purpose.
我也和兰德尔聊过这个，他说这完全是故意的。

612
00:27:52.520 --> 00:28:00.240
He planted a guy standing at reception in the same way that Jim would always stand at reception and talk to Pam.
他特意安排了一个人站在前台，就像吉姆以前总是站在前台和帕姆聊天那样。

613
00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:06.520
He even has his sleeves rolled up the way Jim used to roll up his sleeves.
他甚至把袖子卷起来的方式都和吉姆以前卷袖子的方式一模一样。

614
00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:07.500
Sleeve gate.
袖子门事件。

615
00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.720
Susan, are you listening to this, Susan?
苏珊，你在听吗，苏珊？

616
00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:10.180
Yeah.
在听。

617
00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:10.940
I know.
我知道。

618
00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:12.360
Susan who broke down the sleeves.
就是那个拆解袖子细节的苏珊。

619
00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:13.480
Yeah.
没错。

620
00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:20.720
And Randall said that was on purpose to keep that Pam-Jim thing sort of in our orbit.
兰德尔说那是故意的，为了让我们依然能感受到帕姆和吉姆那种氛围。

621
00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:22.020
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

622
00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.280
That's such a beautiful detail.
这细节真是太美好了。

623
00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:28.160
So Stanford has their own version of Jim and Pam that hang out at reception and flirt.
所以斯坦福也有他们自己的吉姆和帕姆版本，会在前台闲逛调情。

624
00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:28.920
Yeah.
是啊。

625
00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:31.040
Amazing, right?
很神奇吧？

626
00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:31.940
That's amazing.
太神奇了。

627
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:33.260
All right.
好了。

628
00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.540
So let's go back to the Scranton branch.
那我们回到斯克兰顿分部吧。

629
00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.360
Pam is keeping notes on Michael's day.
帕姆在记录迈克尔的一天。

630
00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:42.460
He's on a phone call, and he's doing a Bill Cosby impression.
他在打电话，还模仿了比尔·考斯比。

631
00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:49.060
So Pam holds up her log, and she has written Cosby impression.
帕姆举起她的记录表，上面写着“模仿考斯比”。

632
00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:53.260
So first of all, that is not my handwriting on the log, people asked.
首先，记录表上的字迹不是我的，有人问过这个问题。

633
00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.120
And also, we got a fan question from Jonathan Barak.
另外，我们收到了乔纳森·巴拉克的一个粉丝提问。

634
00:28:56.280 --> 00:29:00.500
When Jan gives Pam a timesheet to write down everything Michael does throughout the day,
当简让帕姆写下迈克尔一天中做的所有事情时，

635
00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:02.420
why doesn't Pam cover for him?
为什么帕姆不帮他打掩护呢？

636
00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:07.400
Like, why not write down sales call instead of Cosby impression?
比如，为什么不写“销售电话”而不是“模仿考斯比”？

637
00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:08.080
Hmm.
嗯。

638
00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:09.640
What are our thoughts on that?
对此你们怎么看？

639
00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:13.120
I mean, Pam sometimes is a little bit of a stickler.
我是说，帕姆有时候有点死板。

640
00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:15.580
She's a rule follower, you know?
她是个守规矩的人，你知道吧？

641
00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:17.020
Yeah.
没错。

642
00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:22.040
I mean, I think if she covers for Michael and then Jan finds out otherwise,
我觉得如果她帮迈克尔打掩护，结果被简发现了，

643
00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:23.940
Pam's going to get in trouble.
帕姆会有麻烦的。

644
00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:25.400
Yeah.
是啊。

645
00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.420
I mean, I feel like she does cover for him later.
我觉得她后来确实帮他打掩护了。

646
00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:31.880
So maybe she starts out being like, all right, I'll do what Jan told me.
所以也许她一开始想的是，好吧，我会照简说的做。

647
00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:36.640
But then she feels bad or something because Michael is such a train wreck for the whole day.
但后来她觉得过意不去之类的，因为迈克尔那一整天简直是一团糟。

648
00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:37.520
Yeah.
没错。

649
00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.620
And he ends up making a huge sale in the end.
而且他最后还做成了一笔大生意。

650
00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:43.980
So that's the thing about Michael, right?
这就是迈克尔的特点，对吧？

651
00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:48.680
Like, his weird ways, they turn out.
就像他那些古怪的行事风格，结果证明还挺管用。

652
00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.480
He's able to connect with people.
他确实有办法和人打成一片。

653
00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:51.160
He is.
确实如此。

654
00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:52.740
Say what you want to say about Michael.
不管你怎么评价迈克尔。

655
00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:54.260
People like him.
大家还是喜欢他的。

656
00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:56.140
Not that have to work with him.
当然，不用跟他一起工作的人是这样。

657
00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:56.760
Yeah.
没错。

658
00:29:57.140 --> 00:29:58.500
Acquaintances really like him.
点头之交确实挺喜欢他的。

659
00:29:58.860 --> 00:29:59.060
All right.
好吧。

660
00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:00.760
Now we're back at the Stanford branch.
现在我们回到斯坦福分部。

661
00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:06.300
And Andy, I guess, has told Karen he'll try to help her, you know, get that chair back.
我想安迪已经告诉凯伦，他会试着帮她，你知道的，把那个职位拿回来。

662
00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:09.300
So he's like, Jim, can you come talk to me over here?
所以他就说，吉姆，你能过来跟我谈谈吗？

663
00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:11.200
And he's like, I can talk to you from here.
吉姆回他，我就在这儿跟你谈就行。

664
00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:13.280
And he's like, yeah, but could you get up and come over here?
然后安迪就说，行吧，但你能起身过来一下吗？

665
00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:14.740
And Karen's like, that's all you got?
凯伦就说，你就这点本事？

